{"content":"🚨 **BLOCKING: the scan flags a required context as CANCELLED even when a NEWER run already re-stamped it green** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

`cancelledRequiredContexts` treats the context as cancelled if **any** check run in the commit's rollup reads `CANCELLED`:

```js
rollup.forEach(function (r) {
    var name = r && (r.name || r.context);
    var concl = r && r.conclusion ? String(r.conclusion).toUpperCase() : null;
    if (name && concl === 'CANCELLED' && contexts.indexOf(name) !== -1) {
        cancelled[name] = true;
    }
});
```

`github_get_commit_check_runs` returns **all** check runs on the commit (verified live: "Get all check runs ... for a commit SHA") — including stale ones from earlier attempts/dispatches. Branch protection resolves each required context by its **latest** check run; this code does not, despite the comment claiming "the check-runs rollup is the source of truth".

Reachable sequence with this repo's own machinery:

1. Validation run A (attempt 1) on head H is cancelled — exactly the #632/#682 class this rule targets (or stale-run cleanup).
2. `revalidate-armed` re-dispatches a fresh run B on H after the 15-min grace → B re-stamps `static`/`gate`/`agents-gate` **green**.
3. Next tick this rule runs **first** (before `merge-validated`), sees A's stale `CANCELLED` check runs, and `gh run rerun`s A + posts the marker — on a head that is already green and mergeable.

Consequences: the merge is delayed a full validation cycle; if the spurious rerun fails (moved base, flake), the freshly re-stamped red hands the PR to `fail_validation` and it gets parked — the rule actively harms the PRs it protects. Same trap after the rule's own remedy: attempt-1 cancelled check runs persist next to attempt-2's, so any tick where the marker read errors (`catch` → `rcReported = false`) re-fires.

Fix (~5 lines): keep only the **latest** check run per required context (max `completed_at`, tie-break by `id`) before testing `=== 'CANCELLED'`, and add a test fixture with a stale-cancelled + fresh-green pair (the current fixtures only ever carry the cancelled runs, which pins the bug).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: once-per-head marker is posted even when the rerun set covers only part of the cancelled contexts** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

`rcTargets` can legitimately cover a **subset** of `rcCancelled`: contexts whose cancelled runs are already attempt-2+ (skipped by design), or whose job names don't match any walked run (`jobs?per_page=50` cap, job-name/context drift). The marker is then posted anyway, and from the next tick on the `rcReported` check short-circuits **before** the mapping probe — the head is "remedied" forever while the uncovered contexts still read `CANCELLED` (the exact gh-682 BLOCKED symptom), and even the per-tick `"no rerunnable ... skip"` warn disappears into the marker.

```js
if (rcReported) {
    console.log('  ⏭️  ' + key + ' cancelled checks already re-run for head — waiting on the rerun');
    continue;
}
```

Fix: post the marker only when `rcTargets` covers **all** of `rcCancelled` (or list the uncovered contexts in the marker text), so an unmapped context keeps visibly retrying/warning instead of being silently latched shut by a partial remediation. Test to add: two cancelled contexts where only one maps to an attempt-1 run → no marker on that tick.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment2.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: copy-paste bug in the new test helper — `it[k] = extra` drops the `[k]` index** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

```js
function prItem682(n, extra) {
    var it = { key: 'pr-' + n, labels: ['ai_validating'], issueNumber: null,
               prNumber: n, draft: false, branch: 'ai/gh-1202',
               pr: { headSha: '898efd4' } };
    if (extra) { for (var k in extra) { if (extra.hasOwnProperty(k)) it[k] = extra; } }
    return it;
}
```

Every sibling helper in this file does `it[k] = extra[k]` (lines 855, 1682, 1864, 2041) — this copy assigns the whole `extra` object to every key. It is latent today only because every call site is `prItem682(1202)`; the first caller that passes `extra` (e.g. `prItem682(1202, { labels: ['ai_validated'] })`) gets `{ labels: {labels: [...]}, prNumber: {labels: [...]}, ... }` and a silently wrong fixture — which also blocks the `ai_validated`-latch test case the suite will need for the stale-run fix. One-character fix.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `number:` vs `pullRequestId:` — keep one spelling per tool in this file** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

This new call site passes `number: ticket.prNumber` to `github_get_pr_comments`, while the other 3 call sites in this file (1584→1587, 1828→1831, 3013→3015) use `pullRequestId:`. Both aliases work against the current dmtools runtime (verified live), but if the tool ever tightens its input schema, every site should already agree — `pullRequestId` is the canonical name per `js/common/trackers.js` ("the number argument keeps the tool's canonical pullRequestId name"). Same payload, just match the file's existing contract. (`github_create_comment` sites in this file do use `number:` — only the `github_get_pr_comments` spelling needs aligning.)
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment4.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `rcRunIds` is dead code** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

```js
var rcRunIds = [];
var rcSeen = {};
rcTargets.forEach(function (t) {
    if (rcSeen[t.runId]) return;
    rcSeen[t.runId] = true;
    rcRunIds.push(t.runId);
```

`rcRunIds` is populated and never read — `rcSeen` alone performs the per-run dedup (and the log line below re-derives the summary from `rcTargets`). Drop the array; less state to misread in an already guard-dense block.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: both probes cap at `per_page=50` — busy heads can silently exceed the window** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

`headWorkflowRuns` (this line) and the per-run jobs probe (line 2785) stop at 50. A long-lived PR head accumulates runs on every push × workflow (the gh-682 evidence alone shows 8+ kicker runs in one 36-second wave), and `actions/runs?head_sha=` returns newest-first — so on a sufficiently busy head the cancelled run can fall outside the first page. Failure mode is quiet: the context still reads `CANCELLED` via the check-runs endpoint, but `rerunTargetsForCancelledContexts` finds no run → `⚠️ ... no rerunnable (attempt-1) cancelled run — skip, retry next tick` forever (no remedy, no marker). Either raise/page the cap for this targeted probe (a filtered `runs?head_sha=` + `conclusion=cancelled` server-side would shrink the payload too), or log the total_count when the window is saturated so the gap is visible.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment6.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `limit: 1` drains a cancel-wave one PR per 10-min tick** _(re-posted: unaddressed at head 7b495d75)_

The action itself is cheap (a few API reads + an idempotent `gh run rerun` — no workflow dispatch, no budget competition), but the gh-682 evidence shows eight branches' kickers cancelled inside one 36-second wave. At `limit: 1` on the 10-minute SM cadence that wave needs ~80 minutes to drain, with `ai_validated` queue-heads stuck BLOCKED the whole time — the dead-zone cost this PR is trying to eliminate. The sibling bulk rules use `limit: 10` (`sweep-stale-validating`, `silent-update-behind`); consider matching them, the guards (marker, in-flight probe, attempt-1-only) already bound the write side.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment7.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `j.step` fallback can never match — run-jobs objects have no `step` property**

```js
var names = (jobs.jobs || []).map(function (j) {
    return j && (j.name || j.step); // job name == check context
});
```

`GET /actions/runs/{id}/jobs` job objects carry `name` (and a nested `steps` **array**), never a scalar `step` — so `j.step` is always `undefined` and the fallback is dead. If the intent was matrix/step-level robustness it doesn't do anything; if not, drop it (`j && j.name` suffices). Harmless today, but a reader will assume the fallback does something.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment8.md"}

